---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArchitectureExplorer from '../components/ArchitectureExplorer.astro';
import { publicArchitectureCounts } from '../lib/publicArchitecture';

const architectureCounts = publicArchitectureCounts();
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Architecture — LUNA"
  description={`Explore LUNA's public architecture: ${architectureCounts.nodes} interacting subsystems around a focused AI model inference region, with reviewed maturity and plain-English descriptions.`}
>
  <section class="page-hero architecture-hero">
    <div class="shell">
      <p class="eyebrow">EXPLORE LUNA'S ARCHITECTURE</p>
      <h1>LUNA is not one AI model.</h1>
      <p class="section-lede">
        LUNA is an architecture of interacting systems built around model inference. Explore the
        systems that handle memory, attention, evaluation, continuity, goals, development, and more.
      </p>
      <div class="status-note">
        <strong>A current public capability map:</strong> every subsystem and maturity label below comes from a reviewed static export of the implemented architecture. It is a public explanation, not a live view into LUNA.
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section architecture-explorer-section">
    <div class="shell shell-wide">
      <ArchitectureExplorer />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section architecture-reading-guide">
    <div class="shell">
      <p class="eyebrow">HOW TO READ THE MAP</p>
      <h2>Start with the architecture. Use maturity as context.</h2>
      <div class="map-reading-grid">
        <article><span class="reading-icon family-icon" aria-hidden="true"></span><h3>Family color</h3><p>Color groups subsystems by the kind of work they do. It does not rank their quality or importance.</p></article>
        <article><span class="reading-icon relationship-icon" aria-hidden="true"></span><h3>Relationships</h3><p>Lines show only the reviewed public connections. Select a subsystem to make its immediate neighborhood clearer.</p></article>
        <article><span class="reading-icon maturity-icon" aria-hidden="true"></span><h3>Maturity</h3><p>The inspector and directory describe development scope without turning the map into a field of status warnings.</p></article>
        <article><span class="reading-icon inference-icon" aria-hidden="true"></span><h3>AI inference</h3><p>The dashed region marks where model inference connects to LUNA. The rest of the map shows the surrounding architecture.</p></article>
      </div>

      <div class="maturity-guide" aria-labelledby="maturity-guide-heading">
        <div class="maturity-guide__intro">
          <p class="eyebrow">PUBLIC MATURITY</p>
          <h3 id="maturity-guide-heading">These labels describe development scope, not quality.</h3>
        </div>
        <dl>
          <div><dt>Working</dt><dd>Functioning today.</dd></div>
          <div><dt>Working + Growing</dt><dd>Already works today and is still being expanded.</dd></div>
          <div><dt>Experimental</dt><dd>A real implementation being used for controlled research or testing.</dd></div>
          <div><dt>Architectural</dt><dd>Part of LUNA's architecture but not currently implemented.</dd></div>
        </dl>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell">
      <p class="eyebrow">PROGRESSIVE RECALL</p>
      <h2>The past becomes progressively available.</h2>
      <p class="section-lede">
        LUNA separates long-term storage from current cognitive availability. Detailed history can remain dormant, compact cues can keep selected recent or unresolved material easy to reach, multiple candidates can become near the surface, and only a very small amount crosses into foreground reasoning.
      </p>

      <div class="architecture-map" style="padding:0;overflow:hidden;background:#051122;border-color:#173a68">
        <img src="/assets/research/progressive-recall-architecture.svg" alt="Public progressive recall architecture showing long-term history, recent and relevant surface, cognitive availability, a bounded foreground workspace, and Sleeve reasoning." style="display:block;width:100%" />
      </div>

      <div class="comparison-grid" style="margin-top:30px">
        <article class="comparison-card">
          <h3>Stack-side cognition</h3>
          <p>Persistent memory, recent-life cueing, activation, limited competition, and selected admission can shape what reaches inference. The Stack is therefore more than passive storage around a model.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="comparison-card luna">
          <h3>Sleeve-side inference</h3>
          <p>The active model reasons over the resulting foreground state. It does not need to own the entire persistent memory system or make every cognitive-state decision itself.</p>
        </article>
      </div>

      <div class="status-note">
        <strong>Current capability cutaway:</strong> the Recent-Life Horizon is Working. Long-Term Memory, the Cognitive Activation Field, and Reverie are Working + Growing; the implemented Reverie path can contribute zero or one eligible internally surfaced memory to the foreground without a language-model call deciding admission.
      </div>
      <div class="page-actions">
        <a class="button button-primary" href="/research/progressive-recall">Read the Progressive Recall technical cutaway</a>
        <a class="button" style="border-color:#bac8d8" href="/research/memory-can-remind-memory">Explore associative cognition</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section-dark">
    <div class="shell">
      <p class="eyebrow">ONE OF THE CORE DISTINCTIONS</p>
      <h2>Retrieval is not development.</h2>
      <p class="section-lede">
        A system may perfectly preserve an experience and still remain behaviorally identical to a version that never had it. LUNA treats storage, retrieval, causal use, and developmental integration as different measurements.
      </p>
      <div class="principle-grid">
        <div class="principle-card"><h3>Storage</h3><p>Did the event enter durable autobiographical history?</p></div>
        <div class="principle-card"><h3>Retrieval</h3><p>Can the event or its meaning become available again when relevant?</p></div>
        <div class="principle-card"><h3>Causal use</h3><p>Did available history actually alter current reasoning, attention, creation, or choice?</p></div>
        <div class="principle-card"><h3>Development</h3><p>Can consequences remain active even when the original episode is not consciously retrieved?</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="page-actions">
        <a class="button button-primary" href="/research/when-memory-is-not-enough">Read the first public observation</a>
        <a class="button button-secondary" href="/research/remembering-is-not-becoming">Explore developmental imprinting</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell prose">
      <p class="eyebrow">STACK & SLEEVE</p>
      <h2>Continuity is intended to survive the model.</h2>
      <p>
        LUNA uses a simple conceptual separation. The <strong>Stack</strong> is the persistent identity-and-continuity state: autobiographical history, durable dispositions, goals, relationships, intentions, history tracking, and other longitudinal state. The <strong>Sleeve</strong> is the active computational system through which that state currently runs.
      </p>
      <p>
        The research objective is that changing provider, model, machine, or embodiment should not require recreating the persistent instance from a prompt. The inference model is therefore important, but it is not intended to be the whole identity of the system.
      </p>
      <div class="callout">The sleeve can change. The Stack persists.</div>

      <h2>Architecture before theatrical behavior.</h2>
      <p>
        LUNA does not treat a model saying “I remember,” “I want,” or “I changed” as proof that the corresponding architecture exists. Claims about memory, motivation, self-modeling, or development are separated from fluent self-description and should ultimately be traceable to system state, history, and controlled experiments.
      </p>
      <p>
        That is why public material distinguishes <strong>implemented behavior</strong>, <strong>accepted architecture</strong>, <strong>observation</strong>, <strong>interpretation</strong>, and <strong>prediction</strong> rather than blending them into one marketing claim.
      </p>

      <div class="reading-path">
        Next: <a href="/research/progressive-recall">see progressive recall in detail</a> · <a href="/chronoforge">see how ChronoForge turns developmental experience into an experimental variable →</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .architecture-hero { padding-bottom: 74px; }
  .architecture-explorer-section { padding-top: 74px; background: linear-gradient(160deg, #06162c, #040e1d); }
  .shell-wide { width: min(calc(100% - 40px), 1520px); }
  .architecture-reading-guide { background: #eef3f8; }
  .map-reading-grid { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(4, 1fr); gap: 16px; margin-top: 46px; }
  .map-reading-grid article { padding: 25px; border: 1px solid #d5e0ec; border-radius: 16px; background: rgba(255,255,255,.8); }
  .map-reading-grid h3 { margin: 17px 0 7px; font-size: 1.08rem; }
  .map-reading-grid p { margin: 0; color: #6c7a8a; font-size: .92rem; }
  .reading-icon { display: block; width: 42px; height: 30px; }
  .family-icon { border: 2px solid #57d5ef; border-radius: 10px; background: rgba(87,213,239,.08); }
  .relationship-icon { position: relative; border-top: 2px solid #6c8fb7; transform: translateY(15px); }
  .relationship-icon::after { content: ""; position: absolute; top: -5px; right: 0; border: 4px solid transparent; border-left-color: #6c8fb7; }
  .maturity-icon { width: auto; color: #1665f2; font-size: .72rem; font-weight: 800; letter-spacing: .08em; }
  .maturity-icon::after { content: "WORKING + GROWING"; }
  .inference-icon { border: 2px dashed #ff9b75; border-radius: 13px; background: rgba(255,155,117,.08); }
  .maturity-guide { display: grid; grid-template-columns: .8fr 1.2fr; gap: 50px; margin-top: 58px; padding: 34px; color: #fff; border-radius: 20px; background: #07162b; }
  .maturity-guide__intro h3 { margin: 0; font-size: 1.55rem; line-height: 1.25; }
  .maturity-guide dl { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; gap: 22px; margin: 0; }
  .maturity-guide dl > div { border-left: 2px solid #2e6cb5; padding-left: 14px; }
  .maturity-guide dt { color: #fff; font-weight: 750; }
  .maturity-guide dd { margin: 4px 0 0; color: #aebfd2; font-size: .9rem; }
  @media (max-width: 960px) {
    .map-reading-grid { grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; }
    .maturity-guide { grid-template-columns: 1fr; gap: 28px; }
  }
  @media (max-width: 620px) {
    .shell-wide { width: min(calc(100% - 20px), 1520px); }
    .map-reading-grid,
    .maturity-guide dl { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
    .maturity-guide { padding: 26px 22px; }
  }
</style>
